    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_01L4xqgQTsqkdfuRbp2GwQ3y",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "facts": [
                  "F4",
                  "F6",
                  "S1:F12",
                  "S1:F13",
                  "F41",
                  "S3:F16",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "S3:F33",
                  "S2:F34",
                  "S2:F40",
                  "F47"
                ],
                "ref": "N1",
                "statement": "In the days after the US-Russia agreement the UK backed the US-led peace effort but did not ease off: it tightened oil and LNG sanctions and sped up air-defence deliveries. That reads as hedging against an energy ceasefire that had no confirmed start date while Russia was still striking Ukraine's energy infrastructure.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "F74",
                  "S16:F72",
                  "S16:F86",
                  "S16:F93",
                  "S17:F82",
                  "S16:F83"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "statement": "Andy Burnham's offer to Holyrood in his first weeks as Prime Minister already has one concrete result, the matched £10 million for defence colleges, and the National Economic Council meeting with the First Ministers, due within weeks, will be the first test of whether bigger offers such as DWP devolution are taken up.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S10:F55",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "S16:F93"
                ],
                "ref": "N3",
                "statement": "Putting Great British Railways' headquarters and the £1 billion northern train fleet both in Derby, alongside the creation of Number 10 North, points to a deliberate policy of placing government institutions and spending outside London.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F6",
                  "F4",
                  "S1:F12"
                ],
                "text": "The week's Ukraine news is best read against the US-Russia agreement of 9 October. London's response backed the US-led peace effort but promised to keep pressure on Russia through sanctions. President Trump then announced a reciprocal energy ceasefire."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F13",
                  "F41"
                ],
                "text": "That ceasefire had no confirmed start date. Ukraine said it would comply as soon as one was set, and as at 13 October Russia was still bombarding Kyiv and its energy infrastructure."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S3:F16",
                  "S3:F38",
                  "S3:F33",
                  "S2:F34",
                  "S2:F40",
                  "F47"
                ],
                "text": "So the UK went further, not softer. Its new sanctions cover over 90% of Russia's oil-producing capacity, it says it will fully implement a maritime services ban on Russian LNG, and ministers have been told to draw up more packages. It is also bringing forward £10 million of a £45 million missile package before winter."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "Taken together, this looks like hedging: backing Washington's diplomacy while building up leverage in case the ceasefire does not hold."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F74",
                  "S16:F72",
                  "S16:F93",
                  "S16:F86"
                ],
                "text": "At home, Andy Burnham is still in his first weeks as Prime Minister, and he has made an early pitch to Holyrood. That includes a new Number 10 North and an offer to devolve DWP employment support to Scotland."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S17:F82",
                  "S16:F83",
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "One concrete result has already come: Edinburgh is matching £10 million for two defence technical colleges. The real test is the National Economic Council meeting with the First Ministers of Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, due in a couple of weeks."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S10:F50",
                  "S10:F55",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "text": "The rail announcements fit the same pattern. Derby gets Great British Railways' headquarters, with up to 450 staff, and Alstom's Derby site will build the £1 billion northern fleet, which points to a deliberate policy of placing institutions and spending outside London."
              }
            ],
            "stories": []
          }
        }
      ],
